ina.\n(784)Department of Intelligent Medical Engineering, Anhui Medical University, \nAnhui, China.\n(785)Department of Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital of Anhui Medical \nUniversity, Hefei, Anhui, China.\n(786)Trauma Research Center, Shiraz University of Medical Sciences, Shiraz, \nIran.\n(787)Department of Public Health Administration, Linyi People's Hospital, Linyi, \nChina.\n(788)Manipal College of Nursing, Manipal Academy of Higher Education, Udupi, \nIndia.\n(789)Department of Epidemiology, Xuzhou Medical University, Xuzhou, China.\n(790)Department of Pharmacy, Bahir Dar University, Bahir Dar, Ethiopia.\n(791)Department of Biostatistics, University of Toyama, Toyama, Japan.\n(792)Department of Public Health, Juntendo University, Tokyo, Japan.\n(793)Cancer Institute, Hacettepe University, Ankara, T\u00fcrkiye.\n(794)Sant'Elia Hospital, University of Catania, Caltanissetta, Italy.\n(795)Department of Bioengineering and Therapeutical Sciences, University of \nCalifornia San Francisco, San Francisco, CA, USA.\n(796)Department of Administration, PGxAI, San Francisco, CA, USA.\n(797)Department of Clinical Practice, Northern Border University, Rafha, Saudi \nArabia.\n(798)Department of Public Health, University of Hail, Hail, Saudi Arabia.\n(799)Department of Zoology and Entomology, Al-Azhar University, Cairo, Egypt.\n(800)Institute of Diagnostic and Interventional Radiology and Neuroradiology, \nUniversity of Duisburg-Essen, Essen, Germany.\n(801)Department of Internal Medicine, Jacobi Medical Center, Bronx, NY, USA.\n(802)Department of Internal Medicine, Albert Einstein College of Medicine, \nBronx, NY, USA.\n(803)Department of Health Management, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical \nUniversity, Shenyang, China.\n(804)Jockey Club School of Public Health and Primary Care, The Chinese \nUniversity of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China.\n(805)Atchabarov Scientific- Research Institute of Fundamental and Applied \nMedicine, Kazakh National Medical University, Almaty, Kazakhstan.\n(806)Clinical Research Centre, An-Najah National University Hospital, Nablus, \nPalestine.\n(807)Department of Pediatrics, Yonsei University, Seoul, South Korea.\n(808)Department of Pediatrics, Kyung Hee University, Seoul, South Korea.\n(809)Faculty of Science and Engineering, Anglia Ruskin University, Cambridge, \nUK.\n\nBACKGROUND/AIMS: This study aims to evaluate the global burden of adverse \neffects of medical treatment (AEMT) using data from the Global Burden of Disease \nStudy (GBD) 2021.\nMETHODS: Data were extracted from the GBD 2021, covering 204 \ncountries/territories from 1990 to 2021. AEMT was defined using ICD-9 and ICD-10 \ncodes, encompassing complications from medical procedures, treatments, or \nhealthcare exposures. Estimates were categorized into fatal and non-fatal \noutcomes and stratified by age, sex, year, and covariates, including the \nSocio-demographic Index (SDI). Mortality-incidence ratios (MIRs), defined as the \nratio of mortality calculated by dividing the number of deaths by the total \nincident cases, were analyzed.\nRESULTS: In 2021, the global age-standardized prevalence, incidence, \ndisability-adjusted life years (DALYs), and mortality rates of AEMT were 11.48 \n(95% uncertainty interval [UI], 8.86-14.13), 150.44 (131.19-171.81), 64.19 \n(51.06-73.11), and 1.53 (1.29-1.68) per 100,000 population, respectively. DALY \nrates were highest in the early neonatal group (4,789.47 per 100,000 population \n[95% UI, 3,682.00-5,963.30]), while mortality rates followed a U-shaped pattern \nacross age groups. In 2021, MIRs were highest at both ends of the age range: the \nearly neonatal group (0.58 [95% UI, 0.55-0.58]) and the 95+ age group (0.05 \n[0.04-0.06]). This pattern was consistent across all SDI quintiles, with higher \nMIRs observed in lower SDI quintiles.\nCONCLUSION: The significantly higher prevalence and incidence rates of AEMT \namong the older population in high SDI quintiles, compared to lower SDI \nquintiles, could be attributed to the healthcare overutilization, highlighting \nthe need for policy adjustments.\n\nDOI: 10.3904/kjim.2025.278\nPMCID: PMC12999252\nPMID: 41850226 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: Conflicts of interest Jae Il Shin reports other \nfinancial or non-financial interests with the Yonsei Fellowship, funded by Youn \nJae Lee (JIS) outside the submitted work. Competing interests for the GBD 2021 \nAEMT Collaborators are listed in Supplementary Appendix (p. 46\u201352)."